<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sam Bellamy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1689–1717</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Black Sam Bellamy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sam Bellamy, known as Black Sam, was an English pirate who in 1716–1717 became one of the most successful raiders of the Golden Age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Piracy</a>, operating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. He began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-hornigold">Benjamin Hornigold</a> and in 1717 captured the slave ship Whydah, which became his flagship. Weeks later the Whydah was wrecked in a storm off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a>, drowning him and most of his crew; the wreck was found in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sam-bellamy.jpg" alt="Sam Bellamy" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geo. S. Harris and Sons / Allen &amp;amp; Ginter · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Bellamy,_Wreck_of_the_Whydah,_from_the_Pirates_of_the_Spanish_Main_series_(N19)_for_Allen_%26_Ginter_Cigarettes_MET_DP835027.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
